omato paste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-espagnole-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espagnole_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Roux","ingredient/roux"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Brioche","dish/brioche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Calisson","dish/calisson"],["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Cervelle de canut","dish/cervelle-de-canut"],["Cream of sorrel soup","dish/cream-of-sorrel-soup"],["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"],["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"],["Crudités","dish/crudites"],["Damien Pignolet","dish/damien-pignolet"],["Daube","dish/daube"],["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"],["Duxelles","dish/duxelles"],["Fin gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Garbure","dish/garbure"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"],["Hollandaise sauce","dish/hollandaise-sauce"],["Kig ha farz","dish/kig-ha-farz"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ed/Sauce_Spagnole-09.JPG/500px-Sauce_Spagnole-09.JPG","w":500,"h":373,"by":"Tamorlan","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sauce_Spagnole-09.JPG"}},"ingredient/gravy":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/gravy","n":"Gravy","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Gravy is a sauce made from the juices of meats and vegetables that run naturally during cooking and often thickened with thickeners for added texture. The gravy may be further coloured and flavoured with gravy salt (a mix of salt and caramel food colouring), gravy browning (gravy salt dissolved in water), or bouillon cubes. Powders can be used as a substitute for natural meat or vegetable extracts. Canned and instant gravies are also available. Gravy is commonly served with roasts, meatloaf, sandwiches, rice, noodles, fries (chips), mashed potatoes, or biscuits (North America; see biscuits and gravy)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-gravy"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravy"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Minnesotan cuisine","cuisine/minnesotan-cuisine",[["Hot beef commercial","dish/hot-beef-commercial"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes","dish/zurcher-geschnetzeltes"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Pot roast","ingredient/pot-roast"],["White bread","ingredient/white-bread"],["Rösti","ingredient/rosti"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/88/Wine_sauces_on_the_sauceboat.jpg/500px-Wine_sauces_on_the_sauceboat.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Peachyeung316","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wine_sauces_on_the_sauceboat.jpg"}},"ingredient/sweet-bean-sauce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sweet-bean-sauce","n":"Sweet bean sauce","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Tianmian sauce, also known as sweet bean sauce, sweet flour sauce or sweet wheat paste, is a thick, smooth, dark brown or black paste with either a mild, savory or sweet flavor. It is commonly used in Northern Chinese cuisine, Northeastern Chinese cuisine, and Korean-Chinese cuisine. Peking duck and jajangmyeon are two popular dishes that feature the sauce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sweet-bean-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_bean_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Jjajangmyeon","dish/jjajangmyeon"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cumian","ingredient/cumian"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/54/Sweetnoodlesauce.jpg/500px-Sweetnoodlesauce.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweetnoodlesauce.jpg"}},"dish/koji-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/koji-food","n":"Kōji (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Kōji is a filamentous fungus, most commonly Aspergillus oryzae, which is traditionally used in Japanese cuisine for the fermentation of food, or a mixture of such a culture with wheat and soybean meal. The latter can be fried and eaten directly or processed to a sauce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-koji-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C5%8Dji_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Aspergillus luchuensis","ingredient/aspergillus-luchuensis"],["Aspergillus niger","ingredient/aspergillus-niger"],["Aspergillus oryzae","ingredient/aspergillus-oryzae"],["Aspergillus sojae","ingredient/aspergillus-sojae"],["Aspergillus tamarii","ingredient/aspergillus-tamarii"],["Aspergillus tubingensis","ingredient/aspergillus-tubingensis"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"],["Sumiso","dish/sumiso"],["Sushi","dish/sushi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9e/Koji_Mold_Rice.jpg/500px-Koji_Mold_Rice.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Zenyrgarden","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koji_Mold_Rice.jpg"}},"ingredient/maraschino-cherry":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/maraschino-cherry","n":"Maraschino cherry","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A maraschino cherry is a preserved, sweetened cherry, typically made from light-colored sweet cherries, such as the Royal Ann, Rainier, or Gold varieties. In their modern form, the cherries are first preserved in a brine solution usually containing sulfur dioxide and calcium chloride to bleach the fruit, then soaked in a suspension of food coloring (common red food dye is Allura Red AC), sugar syrup, and other components. The name and the original fruit is indigenous to Croatia (Croatian: višnja maraska or višnja maraske), originally a luxury delicacy of the  Dalmatian Coast, specifically Zadar, these cherries were dark, sour, and pitted before being steeped in a spirit distilled from the fruit’s own pits, skins, and leaves."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-maraschino-cherry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maraschino_cherry"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine",[["Manchester tart","dish/manchester-tart"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Custard","ingredient/custard"],["Fruit preserves","ingredient/fruit-preserves"],["Shortcrust pastry","ingredient/shortcrust-pastry"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bd/Real_maraschino_cherries.jpg/500px-Real_maraschino_cherries.jpg","w":500,"h":625,"by":"Ilikefood","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Real_maraschino_cherries.jpg"}},"dish/mett":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mett","n":"Mett","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Mett, also known as Hackepeter (Northern Germany, Eastern Germany and Berlin), is a preparation of minced raw pork seasoned with salt and black pepper that is popular in Germany. It is frequently spread on halves of a bread roll, with raw onion optionally on top. Since the 1950s mett has also been offered as a buffet dish decoratively formed into the shape of a hedgehog, with raw onion \"spines\". German law forbids mett being sold with a fat content exceeding 35%."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mett"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mett"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Quick German Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Long-running site by German-born cook Gerhild Fulson (Oma) with traditional German home recipes.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/about-me.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dirndl Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dirndlkitchen.com","note":"Blog of German and Bavarian recipes, from main dishes to holiday baking.","url":"https://dirndlkitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Quick German Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Thuringia page on German-born cook Gerhild Fulson's long-running German recipe site.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/thuringia.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from German cuisine","links":[["Baumkuchen","dish/baumkuchen"],["Bockwurst","dish/bockwurst"],["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chrain","dish/chrain"],["Dampfnudel","dish/dampfnudel"],["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Eintopf","dish/eintopf"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"],["Hamburg steak","dish/hamburg-steak"],["Hedgehog slice","dish/hedgehog-slice"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Labskaus","dish/labskaus"],["Landjäger","dish/landjager"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Mock turtle soup","dish/mock-turtle-soup"],["Potato pancake","dish/potato-pancake"],["Potato salad","dish/potato-salad"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"],["Spaghettieis","dish/spaghettieis"],["Speckkuchen","dish/speckkuchen"],["Spundekäs","dish/spundekas"],["Steckerlfisch","dish/steckerlfisch"],["Stippgrütze","dish/stippgrutze"],["Stollen","dish/stollen"],["Streuselkuchen","dish/streuselkuchen"],["Strudel","dish/strudel"],["Westfälische Rinderwurst","dish/westfalische-rinderwurst"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b7/Mettbroetchen.jpg/500px-Mettbroetchen.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Nize","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mettbroetchen.jpg"}},"ingredient/khoa":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/khoa","n":"Khoa","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Khoa, khoya, khowa or mawa is a soft cheese widely used in South Asian cuisine, encompassing India, Nepal, and Pakistan. It is made of either dried whole milk or milk thickened by heating in an open iron pan. It is lower in moisture than typical fresh cheeses such as ricotta. It is made from whole milk instead of whey."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-khoa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khoa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Hyderabadi cuisine","cuisine/hyderabadi-cuisine",[["Roat","dish/roat"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Refined flour","ingredient/refined-flour"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4d/Khuwa_vendor_nepal.JPG/500px-Khuwa_vendor_nepal.JPG","w":500,"h":332,"by":"Kushal Goyal","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khuwa_vendor_nepal.JPG"}},"dish/turron":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/turron","n":"Turrón","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Spanish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Turrón, torró (Catalan: [tuˈro] / Valencian: [toˈro]) or torrone (Italian: [torˈroːne]) is a Mediterranean nougat confection, typically made of honey, sugar, and egg white, with toasted almonds or other nuts, and usually shaped either into a rectangular tablet or a round cake. Turrón is usually eaten as a dessert food around Christmas in Spain and Italy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-turron"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turr%C3%B3n"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Spanish cuisine","links":[["Andrajos","dish/andrajos"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Buñuelo","dish/bunuelo"],["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Chistorra","dish/chistorra"],["Churro","dish/churro"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Cuajada","dish/cuajada"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Hojuela","dish/hojuela"],["Jamón","dish/jamon"],["Migas","dish/migas"],["Mojo (sauce)","dish/mojo-sauce"],["Patatas a lo pobre","dish/patatas-a-lo-pobre"],["Patatas bravas","dish/patatas-bravas"],["Picatostes","dish/picatostes"],["Pincho","dish/pincho"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Salchichón","dish/salchichon"],["Salmorejo","dish/salmorejo"],["Sobrassada","dish/sobrassada"],["Soldaditos de Pavía","dish/soldaditos-de-pavia"],["Sopa de gato","dish/sopa-de-gato"],["Spanish omelette","dish/spanish-omelette"],["Spanish restaurant","dish/spanish-restaurant"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Tapas","dish/tapas"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5b/Turr%C3%B3n_de_Alicante_%28Casa_Mira%29.jpg/500px-Turr%C3%B3n_de_Alicante_%28Casa_Mira%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Tamorlan","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turr%C3%B3n_de_Alicante_(Casa_Mira).jpg"}}}